module nynd-criminal-local-rules.local-rules

import core.authority.{ CaseOrder }
import core.frcrimp45.{ frcrimp45 }

// The Northern District of New York's own criminal periods, counted under
// the criminal rules (FRCrimP 45(a) governs "any local rule"). No trigger
// carries a service method: FRCrimP 45(c)'s three added days follow the
// manner of service, which is not modeled here.
//
// Periods that are FRCrimP periods stay in lit/frcrimp or are only named
// here: the notice of appeal from a magistrate judge's release or
// detention order is filed within 14 days of its entry under FRCrimP
// 58(g)(2)(A) (N.D.N.Y. L.R. Cr. P. 59.1), and a tracking-warrant return
// is due within 10 days after the device's use ends under FRCrimP
// 41(f)(2)(B) (L.R. Cr. P. 41.1(b)).
//
// Conditions that are not deadlines from an event stay in notes: the
// proposed jury verdict form follows the Criminal Pretrial Scheduling
// Order issued at arraignment (L.R. Cr. P. 31.1), and the presentence
// report, objections, presentence memoranda and sentencing follow the
// Uniform Presentence Order, which the court may modify subject to 18
// U.S.C. § 3552(d) (L.R. Cr. P. 32.1(a), (c)); neither order is vendored.
// A continuance of arraignment to obtain counsel is at most one week at a
// time (L.R. Cr. P. 44.1), a limit on a continuance rather than a period
// from an event. The Clerk serves the judgment within three days after
// sentencing (L.R. Cr. P. 32.1(d)), a duty of the Clerk, not of a party.
// A search warrant return after a delayed notice is due 30 days after the
// delayed-notice deadline expires, and the motion to unseal or the request
// to extend sealing then 30 days after that expiry (L.R. Cr. P. 41.1(b)-
// (d)): periods counted from another deadline, not from an event.
//
// The earlier N.D.N.Y. criminal outline's FRCrimP flow and its decisions
// (United States v. Salerno, Barker v. Wingo, Zedner v. United States,
// Brady v. Maryland and the others it named) are FRCrimP, Speedy Trial
// Act or constitutional authority and belong to lit/frcrimp; they are
// removed here: no reporter text is vendored.
/// A memorandum of law: at most 25 pages unless the party obtains the
/// Court's permission before filing (N.D.N.Y. L.R. Cr. P. 12.1(a)).
limit memorandum-of-law {
  pages <= 25 cite "N.D.N.Y. L.R. Cr. P. 12.1(a)"
} cite "N.D.N.Y. L.R. Cr. P. 12.1(a)"

/// A motion is served (N.D.N.Y. L.R. Cr. P. 12.1(a)).
event MotionServed() paper memorandum-of-law
/// The opposition (response) papers are served (N.D.N.Y. L.R. Cr. P.
/// 12.1(a)).
event ResponseServed() paper memorandum-of-law
/// Reply papers are filed and served (N.D.N.Y. L.R. Cr. P. 12.1(a)). The
/// rule says reply papers "may not exceed (10) pages in length" and bars a
/// sur-reply; the vendored text writes the count only as "(10)", which no
/// page-limit cite binds, so the limit stays in this note.
event ReplyServed()
/// The Court decides a motion (N.D.N.Y. L.R. Cr. P. 12.1(h)).
event DecisionEntered()
/// The defendant is arraigned (N.D.N.Y. L.R. Cr. P. 14.1).
event Arraigned()
/// The date set for the start of jury selection (N.D.N.Y. L.R. Cr. P.
/// 14.1(d)).
event JurySelectionScheduled()
/// The Court orders the presentence report (N.D.N.Y. L.R. Cr. P. 32.1(b)).
event PresentenceReportOrdered()
/// The date set for sentencing (N.D.N.Y. L.R. Cr. P. 32.3).
event SentencingScheduled()
/// A search warrant is executed (N.D.N.Y. L.R. Cr. P. 41.1(b)).
event SearchWarrantExecuted()
/// The search warrant return is filed (N.D.N.Y. L.R. Cr. P. 41.1(c), (d)).
event SearchWarrantReturnFiled()
/// Defense counsel files the notice of appearance (N.D.N.Y. L.R. Cr. P.
/// 44.2(a)).
event AppearanceFiled()
/// The notice of appeal from a magistrate judge's release or detention
/// order, or from a magistrate judge's judgment of conviction in a consent
/// misdemeanor case, is filed (N.D.N.Y. L.R. Cr. P. 59.1).
event MagistrateAppealNoticed()
/// The appellant's brief on such an appeal is submitted (N.D.N.Y. L.R. Cr.
/// P. 59.1).
event MagistrateAppellantBriefFiled()
/// The appellee's brief on an appeal from a release or detention order is
/// submitted (N.D.N.Y. L.R. Cr. P. 59.1(a)).
event MagistrateAppelleeBriefFiled()

/// N.D.N.Y. L.R. Cr. P. 12.1(a): the opposing party files and serves its
/// opposition no more than 21 days after service of the motion, unless the
/// Court orders otherwise.
deadline opposition-period: 21.days after MotionServed() counted_by frcrimp45 delegable {
  to CaseOrder
  op set
} cite "N.D.N.Y. L.R. Cr. P. 12.1(a)"
{
  forum frcrimp
}

/// N.D.N.Y. L.R. Cr. P. 12.1(a): the moving party files and serves any
/// reply no more than seven days after service of the response papers,
/// unless the Court orders otherwise.
deadline reply-period: 7.days after ResponseServed() counted_by frcrimp45 delegable {
  to CaseOrder
  op set
} cite "N.D.N.Y. L.R. Cr. P. 12.1(a)"
{
  forum frcrimp
}

/// N.D.N.Y. L.R. Cr. P. 12.1(h): a motion for reconsideration of a Court
/// order is filed within 14 days of the date of the decision.
deadline reconsideration-period: 14.days after DecisionEntered() counted_by frcrimp45 cite "N.D.N.Y. L.R. Cr. P. 12.1(h)"
{
  forum frcrimp
}

/// N.D.N.Y. L.R. Cr. P. 14.1(b): 14 days after arraignment, or on a date
/// the Court sets for good cause shown, the government makes available the
/// FRCrimP 16(a) information with notice of its intent to use it, Brady
/// material and notice of Federal Rule of Evidence 404(b) evidence.
deadline government-discovery: 14.days after Arraigned() counted_by frcrimp45 delegable {
  to CaseOrder
  op set
} cite "N.D.N.Y. L.R. Cr. P. 14.1(b)"
{
  forum frcrimp
}

/// N.D.N.Y. L.R. Cr. P. 14.1(c): unless the defendant refuses in writing
/// the FRCrimP 16(a)(1)(C)-(E) materials, the defendant makes available all
/// FRCrimP 16(b) information within 21 days of arraignment.
deadline defense-discovery: 21.days after Arraigned() counted_by frcrimp45 cite "N.D.N.Y. L.R. Cr. P. 14.1(c)"
{
  forum frcrimp
}

/// N.D.N.Y. L.R. Cr. P. 14.1(d): no less than 14 days before the start of
/// jury selection, or on a date the Court sets for good cause shown, the
/// government tenders Giglio material and the prior convictions of any
/// informant who will testify.
deadline giglio-disclosure: 14.days before JurySelectionScheduled() counted_by frcrimp45 delegable {
  to CaseOrder
  op set
} cite "N.D.N.Y. L.R. Cr. P. 14.1(d)"
{
  forum frcrimp
}

/// N.D.N.Y. L.R. Cr. P. 32.1(b): defense counsel who wishes to attend the
/// probation officer's interview of the defendant advises the Probation
/// Office within two days of the date the presentence report is ordered.
deadline presentence-interview-notice: 2.days after PresentenceReportOrdered() counted_by frcrimp45 cite "N.D.N.Y. L.R. Cr. P. 32.1(b)"
{
  forum frcrimp
}

/// N.D.N.Y. L.R. Cr. P. 32.3: letters in support of the defendant's
/// character are filed on CM/ECF no later than seven days before the
/// sentencing date to be considered, unless good cause is shown.
deadline letters-in-support: 7.days before SentencingScheduled() counted_by frcrimp45 cite "N.D.N.Y. L.R. Cr. P. 32.3"
{
  forum frcrimp
}

/// N.D.N.Y. L.R. Cr. P. 41.1(b): the search warrant return is filed within
/// 30 days after the execution of the warrant.
deadline search-warrant-return: 30.days after SearchWarrantExecuted() counted_by frcrimp45 cite "N.D.N.Y. L.R. Cr. P. 41.1(b)"
{
  forum frcrimp
}

/// N.D.N.Y. L.R. Cr. P. 41.1(c), (d): in a sealed search warrant case the
/// government files a motion to unseal, or a request to extend the
/// unsealing deadline, no later than 180 days after the search warrant
/// return is filed; the sealing order's expiration may not exceed that
/// unless extended (L.R. Cr. P. 41.1(a)).
deadline search-warrant-unseal: 180.days after SearchWarrantReturnFiled() counted_by frcrimp45 delegable {
  to CaseOrder
  op set
} cite "N.D.N.Y. L.R. Cr. P. 41.1(c); N.D.N.Y. L.R. Cr. P. 41.1(d)"
{
  forum frcrimp
}

/// N.D.N.Y. L.R. Cr. P. 44.2(a): defense counsel's appearance certifies
/// six credit hours of federal criminal defense continuing education in
/// the past two years or, if not, that counsel will complete them within
/// 30 days of filing the notice of appearance.
deadline defense-cle-certification: 30.days after AppearanceFiled() counted_by frcrimp45 cite "N.D.N.Y. L.R. Cr. P. 44.2(a)"
{
  forum frcrimp
}

/// N.D.N.Y. L.R. Cr. P. 59.1(a), (c): unless otherwise ordered, on an
/// appeal from a magistrate judge's release or detention order or from a
/// magistrate judge's judgment in a consent misdemeanor case, the
/// appellant's brief is filed within 14 days following the filing of the
/// notice of appeal.
deadline magistrate-appeal-appellant-brief: 14.days after MagistrateAppealNoticed() counted_by frcrimp45 delegable {
  to CaseOrder
  op set
} cite "N.D.N.Y. L.R. Cr. P. 59.1(a); N.D.N.Y. L.R. Cr. P. 59.1(c)"
{
  forum frcrimp
}

/// N.D.N.Y. L.R. Cr. P. 59.1(a), (c): unless otherwise ordered, the
/// appellee's brief is filed within 14 days following submission of the
/// appellant's brief; the appeal is taken on submission without oral
/// argument unless the Court orders otherwise.
deadline magistrate-appeal-appellee-brief: 14.days after MagistrateAppellantBriefFiled() counted_by frcrimp45 delegable {
  to CaseOrder
  op set
} cite "N.D.N.Y. L.R. Cr. P. 59.1(a); N.D.N.Y. L.R. Cr. P. 59.1(c)"
{
  forum frcrimp
}

/// N.D.N.Y. L.R. Cr. P. 59.1(a): on an appeal from a release or detention
/// order, any reply brief is filed within seven days following submission
/// of the appellee's brief, unless otherwise ordered. The consent
/// misdemeanor appeal (L.R. Cr. P. 59.1(c)) provides for no reply.
deadline magistrate-appeal-reply-brief: 7.days after MagistrateAppelleeBriefFiled() counted_by frcrimp45 delegable {
  to CaseOrder
  op set
} cite "N.D.N.Y. L.R. Cr. P. 59.1(a)"
{
  forum frcrimp
}
